    <img id="backgroundImage" src="" alt="Background Image" style="max-height: 100vh; max-width: 100vw;">
</div>

<button onclick="changeBackgroundColor()">Change Background Color</button>

<script>
function changeBackgroundColor() {
    // Get the current color from local storage or any other method
    const backgroundColor = localStorage.getItem('backgroundColor') || 'white';

    // Set the background color in the body element
    document.body.style.backgroundColor = backgroundColor;

    // Change the background image based on the stored color
    const backgroundImageElement = document.getElementById('backgroundImage');
    if (backgroundColor === 'blue') {
        backgroundImageElement.src = 'path/to/blue-image.jpg';
    } else if (backgroundColor === 'green') {
        backgroundImageElement.src = 'path/to/green-image.jpg';
    } else {
        backgroundImageElement.src = '';
    }
}

// Load the last used background color from local storage
const savedColor = localStorage.getItem('lastUsedColor');
if (savedColor) {
    changeBackgroundColor();
}
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:

1. **HTML Structure**:
   - The `<div>` with the ID `backgroundImageContainer` serves as a container for the background image.
   - An `<img>` tag inside it loads the background image when the script runs.
   - A `<button>` triggers the `changeBackgroundColor()` function.

2. **JavaScript Function (`changeBackgroundColor()`)**:
   - This function retrieves the current background color either from local storage or uses a default value ('white' in this case).
   - It then changes the background color of the entire webpage using `document.body.style.backgroundColor`.
   - Based on the retrieved color, it sets the source of the background image to one of two predefined images.

3. **Storing the Last Used Color**:
   - When the page first loads, the script checks if there's a stored color in local storage and calls `changeBackgroundColor()` with that color.

### Note:
- Ensure that the paths to the images are correct relative to where your HTML file is located.
- Local Storage may not be available in all browsers, so consider fallbacks like setting colors directly in CSS instead.

This setup should allow users to click a button and see different background colors/images appear on their desktop.

